@startuml
' ============================================================================
' METADATA
'   author:       TheRobotDrafts Examples
'   description:  Demonstrates cross-context dependencies, transitive
'                 dependencies, and package generalization across a large
'                 enterprise
'   usecase:      Educational example modeling a CRM + Orders + Billing +
'                 Identity enterprise
'   complexity:   complex
'   domain:       Enterprise SaaS
' ============================================================================
title Example Package Diagram - Enterprise System (Multiple Bounded Contexts)

' ============================================================================
' ENTERPRISE PACKAGE DIAGRAM
' 10-15 packages spanning multiple BOUNDED CONTEXTS (DDD).
' Concepts demonstrated:
'   * Bounded Contexts as top-level packages, each owning sub-packages.
'   * CROSS-CONTEXT dependencies (one context referencing another's published API).
'   * TRANSITIVE dependencies: A -> B -> C means A indirectly depends on C.
'   * PACKAGE GENERALIZATION: a specialized package inherits from a generic one
'     (drawn as a solid line with a hollow triangle, same as class inheritance).
' ============================================================================

' ====================== CONTEXT: Identity & Access ==========================
package "IdentityContext" {
  package "Auth" {
    interface Authenticator
    class OauthProvider
  }
  package "Accounts" {
    class User
    class Tenant
  }
  package "SharedKernel" {
    class AuditableEntity
  }
}

' ============================ CONTEXT: Sales ================================
package "SalesContext" {
  package "Crm" {
    class Lead
    class Opportunity
  }
  package "Quotes" {
    class Quote
    class QuoteLine
  }
}

' =========================== CONTEXT: Fulfillment ===========================
package "FulfillmentContext" {
  package "Orders" {
    class SalesOrder
    class OrderItem
  }
  package "Shipping" {
    class Shipment
    class Carrier
  }
}

' ============================ CONTEXT: Billing ==============================
package "BillingContext" {
  package "Invoicing" {
    class Invoice
  }
  package "Payments" {
    class PaymentProcessor
    class Refund
  }
}

' ============================ CONTEXT: Reporting ============================
package "ReportingContext" {
  package "Analytics" {
    class RevenueReport
  }
  package "Export" {
    class CsvExporter
  }
}

' ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
' CROSS-CONTEXT DEPENDENCIES (dashed). Contexts should depend only on a small
' PUBLISHED INTERFACE of another context - not its internals.
' ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

' Quotes depend on Leads/Opportunities from Sales CRM
Quotes .down.> Crm : <<access>>

' Orders depend on Quotes (convert quote -> order)
Orders .up.> Quotes : <<import>>

' Shipping depends on Orders (ship what was ordered)
Shipping .up.> Orders : <<access>>

' Invoicing depends on Orders (bill what shipped)
Invoicing .left.> Orders : <<access>>

' Payments depends on Invoicing (pay an invoice)
Payments .up.> Invoicing : <<import>>

' ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
' TRANSITIVE DEPENDENCY EXAMPLE:
'   Shipping -> Orders -> Quotes -> Crm
' Shipping indirectly depends on Crm. This is acceptable but should be kept
' shallow; long transitive chains indicate tight coupling between contexts.
' ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

' ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
' CROSS-CUTTING: every context consumes Identity for the current user/tenant.
' Drawn once from each context's outer package to the Auth/Accounts packages.
' ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

SalesContext .up.> Auth : <<access>>
FulfillmentContext .up.> Auth : <<access>>
BillingContext .up.> Auth : <<access>>
ReportingContext .up.> Auth : <<access>>

' ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
' PACKAGE GENERALIZATION (solid hollow-triangle arrowhead).
' "Analytics" and "Export" both specialize the generic "ReportPackage".
' Generalization means the sub-package INHERITS the contents/contracts of the
' generic package and may extend them - just like class inheritance.
' ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

package "ReportPackage" <<abstract>> {
  interface IReportSource
}

Analytics -|> ReportPackage : specializes
Export -|> ReportPackage : specializes

' ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
' SHARED KERNEL reuse: domain entities extend a shared auditable base.
' (class-level detail included to show how the SharedKernel package is reused
'  across contexts - a lighter-weight alternative to generalizing a package.)
' ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
SalesOrder -up-|> SharedKernel.AuditableEntity
Invoice -up-|> SharedKernel.AuditableEntity

note bottom of ReportingContext
  <b>Transitive chain:</b>
  Shipping --> Orders --> Quotes --> Crm
  Keep these shallow to avoid hidden coupling.
end note

@enduml
